From d87205c61bd5236dbfbb6ed163d977bb545f0aac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 18:39:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Guest Portal: create/delete applied, guest isolation, guest
 DNS, a quicker sign-in (102.8 line)

- A Guest Network Pro create sent from the Network page reached the router as "restart_sdn+4" and
  none of it ran; a delete did. httpd turns a '+' back into a space and records the request, and
  reaper_sdn_settle applies the saved list itself when rc ran no guest-network service within the
  settle window (rc/sdn.c). After any guest-network change the portal processes follow the saved
  settings: one portal start per request, a deleted portal network stops its portal, and a portal
  started without a pid file is found by its own configuration argument.
- Guest isolation (rc/reaper_guest.c, new): a guest or Guest Portal device reaches only the WAN or
  its VPN client, and the router only for DHCP, DNS and the portal ports; the layer sits after
  Warden and before Gatekeeper and follows the set of networks. Access Intranet on a portal
  network opens the main LAN only. A VPN binding starts with its Kill Switch on.
- Sign-in: before sign-in the portal answers every A lookup with a stand-in address in the
  benchmarking range, so a phone's sign-in sheet opens even when the network's resolver has no
  upstream. The success page waits for the first address to answer. The portal network's resolver
  binds by interface name, so it starts in either order. After sign-in a device's connections to
  the stand-in are refused at once instead of timing out (about 30 s saved per sign-in), and
  frames the router sends itself on the portal's interface are no longer taken for a guest.
- Guest DNS: every port-53 query from a portal tunnel goes to the guest network's own resolver and
  port 853 is dropped; a guest resolver without intranet access reads no hosts file, so no
  main-LAN names. The network's DNS Server setting picks the guests' upstream: empty means
  Cloudflare for Families over DNS-over-TLS through a forwarder of its own (restarted by the
  watchdog after the clock is set or if it stops), a known DNS-over-TLS provider is asked over
  TLS, any other address plainly. A VPN-bound portal keeps the tunnel's DNS. The Network page says
  so under DNS Server.
- The portal module is handed the stored secret, as stock does - a second decrypt made every
  sign-in fail. A Free Wi-Fi portal is terms-only (no passcode); the Tweaks Guest Portal secret
  panel is gone, each router makes its own.
Also in these files: services.c and rc/Makefile drop the ASUS DDNS client, web.c its unregister
handler (the ASUS DDNS commit).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
